<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 19: 4-Momentum</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> Reading covers sections 8.1 through 8.5. 
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability on paper. When you are done, screenshot your work and upload 
      it as a pdf using the <q>File Upload</q> question below. When you submit the quiz, <alert>don't navigate away from Canvas.</alert> Scroll
      down to the bottom of the page for a download link to the solutions to the quiz.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          		Conceptually, how is the relativistic energy <m>E=\gamma m</m> different from the pre-relativity 
                kinetic energy <m>K=\frac{1}{2} mv^2</m>? Use the discussion of page 223 as a guide 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Briefly explain why the total energy and total momentum for the system of page 224 behaves as a 
            single object with 20 units of mass.
        </p>

      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          		In Chapter 7, we defined the 4-momentum to be 
                <m>\underset{\sim}{p} = m\frac{\text{spacetime displacement}}{\text{proper time}}</m>
                (the undertilde is a notation for 4-D vectors, like the arrow over 3-D vectors).
                Photons are massless particles, so the <q><m>m</m></q> in the definition of the 4-momentum is a 
                problem. Is there another issue in trying to define the 4-momentum as we did in Chapter 7? 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          		Figure 8-6 shows the solution to a collision of a photon of energy <m>2m_e</m> that backscatters 
                off a free electron. Write the conservation of momentum and conservation of energy equations for 
                this collision, keeping the final energy of the photon and the electron as unknowns. 
                There is no need to solve the equations.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The figure in page 234 states that the <q>polyelectron</q> is moving at 
                <m>v=\frac{4}{5}</m>. Explain this result from the information given in that figure.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

  </page>
</worksheet>